/*  starT -- THE STAR LAW. Minted 2026-09-01 (SEQ 111), Tony's ruling.

        *x   reads ONE level.
        **x  is `*` applied TWICE -- composition, NOT a fixpoint.
        a `*` past the leaf REFUSES.
        A fixpoint read is a named call or nothing.

    ⚠ `**` IS NO LONGER AN OPERATOR. It left the Operators table and the
    UnaryOPS bin, and opDerefAll retired with it (zero call sites).

    ⚠⚠ BUT REMOVING THE MERGED OPERATOR DID NOT PRODUCE COMPOSITION. This header
    used to finish that sentence with "so `**x` tokenizes as two unaries", which
    was read off the operator table and is FALSIFIED BY MEASUREMENT (C19, graded
    on addrOf): ONLY ONE STAR IS EVER APPLIED, and N stars behave as exactly one.
    Spaced `* *x` reads identically, so it is not longest match merging them. The
    LAW above is Tony's and stands; the machine does not implement it yet.

    ⚠⚠ SO THE ROW THAT IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT IS S2b, AND ITS DESCRIPTION HERE WAS
    WRONG UNTIL 2026-09-01. S2b is `**x` on a TWO-DEEP pointer and it WANTS THE
    LEAF -- that is what composition means. Under the flip it gets the MIDDLE. So
    when this fixture re-pins at the flip, S2b must go RED and stay red until
    composition works. The name had been relocated to S2b at C19 and the sentence
    around it still described S3a, which is a half-applied edit and reads as a
    ruling nobody made.

    ⚠ S3a WILL READ THE LEAF AT THE FLIP AND WILL BE RIGHT TO -- FOR THE WRONG
    REASON, because one star on a one-deep pointer reaches the leaf by itself.
    Do not read a green S3a as evidence that the second star ran.  */

register(StarT);
define
    stLeaf="LEAF";
    st1;
    st2;
    st3;
    stA; stB; stC; stD; stE; stF;
    stRun code={
        st1 := stLeaf;
        st2 := st1;
        st3 := st2;

        stA = *st1;
        stB = *st2;
        stC = **st2;
        stD = **st1;
        stE = ***st3;
        stF = *stLeaf;

        print "starT S1  *x   one-deep   =" stA "  want stA flip-OFF -- the auto-unwrap overshoots":;
        print "starT S2a *x   two-deep   =" stB "  want stB flip-OFF":;
        print "starT S2b **x  two-deep   =" stC "  want stC flip-OFF":;
        print "starT S3a **x  ONE-deep   =" stD "  want stD -- MUST refuse in BOTH arms":;
        print "starT S3b ***x three-deep =" stE "  want stE flip-OFF":;
        print "starT S4  *x   on a LEAF  =" stF "  want stF -- MUST refuse, ANTI-VACUITY, BOTH arms":;
        };
    ;
stRun();
print "START SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

⚠⚠ EVERY ROW HERE IS FLIP-GATED, AND THE FIXTURE IS PINNED AT ITS FLIP-OFF
VALUES BECAUSE THAT IS THE FLEET'S BASELINE.

With gNoUnwrap OFF every row refuses, and the reason is not the star: the
AUTO-UNWRAP dereferences the operand on the way in, so opDeref receives the LEAF
and its own single step is one too many. The error names the leaf, not the
pointer, which is the tell. Under gNoUnwrap ON the auto-unwrap is gone and the
stars start reading. So this whole fixture goes RED at the flip and re-pins --
deliberately, like holderT row 3.

⚠⚠⚠ S3a IS NOW GRADED, AND THE ANSWER IS NEITHER OPTION ANYBODY OFFERED.
Graded 2026-09-01 (SEQ 113 item 2) on the `addrOf` instrument, which is what the
paragraph below said was missing. THE SUSPICION WAS FALSIFIED AND SOMETHING
LARGER WAS FOUND: there is no fixpoint, and there is no composition either.

    ONLY ONE STAR IS EVER APPLIED. N stars behave as exactly one.

MEASURED, gNoUnwrap = 1, one run, identity by `addrOf`'s body column -- which is
carrier-proof, because the argument carrier mints a fresh FIELD every call but
the BODY survives it:

    baselines      s3Leaf body #2      s3One body #4      s3Two body #6
    R1   *s3One      -> body #2   LEAF      one star, one-deep     LAW HOLDS
    R2   **s3One     -> body #2   LEAF      <- this is S3a
    R3   *s3Two      -> body #4   MIDDLE    one star, two-deep     LAW HOLDS
    R4   **s3Two     -> body #4   MIDDLE    <- THE ROW THAT DECIDES
    R5   * *s3Two    -> body #4   MIDDLE    spaced: no different
    R6   ***s3Two    -> body #4   MIDDLE    a third star: no different

⚠ R4 IS THE DISCRIMINATOR AND R2 ALONE COULD NEVER HAVE BEEN. Both a fixpoint
and a working composition predict `**s3Two` reaches the LEAF (#2). It reaches the
MIDDLE (#4). Only "the second star does nothing" predicts that -- and the same
hypothesis explains R2's leaf without any fixpoint, because one star on a
ONE-deep pointer reaches the leaf all by itself. THE READING THAT LOOKED LIKE A
FIXPOINT WAS A ONE-DEEP COINCIDENCE.

⚠ AND THE ABSENCE IS EVIDENCE HERE, unusually: there is NOT ONE `ERROR unary *`
LINE in the flip-ON run. If a second star were being applied and refusing, it
would say so by name -- F-41 made all seven operators refuse by name for exactly
this reason. Nothing refused, so nothing second ever ran.

⚠ R5 KILLS THE OBVIOUS MECHANISM. `* *x` with a space reads identically, so this
is NOT longest-match merging two stars into one token -- spacing would separate
them. The extra stars are dropped somewhere after tokenizing. WHERE is not
diagnosed and is not guessed at here, per the standing split: reproduction proves
the SYMPTOM, never the CAUSE.

⚠⚠ THIS CONTRADICTS C16/C17's CLAIM THAT "`**x` IS NOW `*` TWICE." The operator
and the bin entry were both removed and that much is certified by the canary --
but removing the merged operator did not produce composition, it produced a
single star. The claim was structural and read from the table; this is measured.

⚠⚠ AND IT RELOCATES THE ROW THAT MATTERS: **S2b, NOT S3a.** S2b is `**x` on a
two-deep pointer wanting the LEAF, and under the flip it gets the MIDDLE. When
this fixture re-pins at the flip, S2b is the row that must go red and stay red
until composition works. S3a will read the leaf and be RIGHT to -- for the wrong
reason, which is why it is written down here rather than left for a reader to
rediscover.

THE PARAGRAPH BELOW IS THE PRE-GRADING STATE, KEPT AS THE REASONING TRAIL. Its
